166	/**
167	 * The MHPCO claim rules: what the office reimburses for a damage report against
168	 * a policy, after the deductible and within the policy's payout cap.
169	 *
170	 * Two decisions the office revises on their own schedules meet here, in the
171	 * order the specification fixes: its clause book states how much of a damage
172	 * counts as reimbursable at all, and only then is the deductible borne. A new
173	 * clause or a revised threshold is the clause book's business; repricing the
174	 * deductible is this rule's.
175	 *
176	 * A payout is a payment out of the office, so its fraction of a G is rounded in
177	 * the office's favor by {@link InOfficesFavor}.
178	 */
179	public class ClaimPolicy {
180	
181	    private static final BigDecimal DEDUCTIBLE_PER_DAMAGE = new BigDecimal("100");
182	    private static final BigDecimal UNRULY_ITEM_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE = new BigDecimal("0.5");
183	
184	    /**
185	     * The office reimburses the reimbursable part of a damage less the
186	     * deductible, never below nothing.
187	     */
188	    public BigDecimal reimbursementFor(BigDecimal damageAmount, InsuredItem damagedItem) {
189	        return InOfficesFavor.payment(reimbursableDamageUnderClauses(damageAmount, damagedItem)
190	                .subtract(DEDUCTIBLE_PER_DAMAGE)
191	                .max(BigDecimal.ZERO));
192	    }
193	
194	    /**
195	     * How much of a damage the office's clause book lets it count as
196	     * reimbursable, read from the damaged item and settled before the
197	     * deductible. The high-enchantment clause reimburses an unruly item's damage
198	     * at a reduced rate; a damage no clause speaks to counts in full.
199	     *
200	     * The office's second clause, that damage to an item of dragon material is
201	     * fully reimbursed, is absent here deliberately rather than by oversight: in
202	     * every case the specification states it grants exactly what a damage no
203	     * clause speaks to already earns. Below the unruly threshold it reimburses in
204	     * full, which is the default; at or above it the specification gives the
205	     * reduced rate precedence. So the clause never changes a reimbursement, and
206	     * the office does not record an item's material at all. A revision that gave
207	     * dragon material its own rate, or precedence over the high-enchantment
208	     * clause, would be the first to make the material worth recording.
209	     */
